 "source": {
  "author": "PingYou Ltd",
  "kind": "new finite construction",
  "attribution_note": "Classical equilateral triangular tiling, proper cube symmetries and guillotine composition. No finite published donor packing. PingYou Ltd supplies the heterogeneous finite whole-strip return words and their complete certificates.",
  "construction": {
   "name": "heterogeneous_complete_triangular_strip_rows_and_layers",
   "proof": "ALTERNATING WHOLE-TRIANGLE STRIP RETURN\n\nThe ordinary bounding cell loses half its area for an equilateral triangle.\nRecover that area by a physical marked return before attempting a metric\nrealization. Put a=1/sqrt(3). The canonical unit-edge triangle has vertices\n(a,0),(-a/2,1/2),(-a/2,-1/2). For index t>=0, use its identity frame when\nt is even and its half-turn about z when t is odd. Put its center at\n((t mod 2)*a/2,t/2,0). Consecutive triangles share a complete oblique edge;\ntheir interiors lie on opposite sides of that edge. Nonconsecutive\ntriangles have disjoint y interiors except possible shared boundary.\nExtruding each complete triangle through the same prism height L keeps\nthe same nonoverlap and all whole-owner identities.\n\nA strip of m such prisms lies in the exact rectangle\n[-a/2,a] by [-1/2,m/2], extruded through [-L/2,L/2]. Its dimensions are\nsqrt(3)/2, (m+1)/2, L. Its midpoint is (a/4,(m-1)/4,0).\nThe additional half-unit of height is the retained pair of end costs;\nit may not be discarded by an infinite-strip density argument.\n\nPut complete strips in disjoint rectangular grid cells using these exact\ndimensions. A grid A by B by C fits in the common cube of side\nmax(A*sqrt(3)/2,B*(m+1)/2,C*L), and contains A*B*C*m complete prisms.\nKeep an explicitly indexed subset of N whole owners. This is a complete\nfinite return, not a marginal planar capacity statement.\n\nThe source is the classical equilateral triangular tiling and Cartesian\ncomposition. PingYou Ltd supplies the finite strip/grid assemblies and\ntheir certificates, without claiming the classical tiling as new. The\nzero-gain ordinary-length branch and every tested finite strip length\nremain useful controls. Native witnesses round the prescribed centers,\napply one common tiny outward dilation, retain the exact two half-turn\nquaternions, and are checked on every actual host wall and owner pair.\n\nHeterogeneous complete triangular-strip rows and layers\n\nUse the alternating strip in TRIANGLE_STRIP_PROOF.txt. A strip of m whole triangular-prism owners has exact dimensions(sqrt(3)/2,(m+1)/2,L), including both end costs. Its centers before subtracting its bounding-box midpoint are((t mod2)*sqrt(3)/6,t/2,0), t=0,...,m-1. Its alternating frames are identity and the z half-turn. The midpoint is(sqrt(3)/12,(m-1)/4,0).\n\nA proper signed cube symmetry acts on the ENTIRE strip, including its centers and alternating source frames. Take six fixed proper axis-permutation representatives. Their box dimensions are the corresponding permutations; no angle parameter is introduced. Unlike the earlier fixed-strip mixed-layer family, a row word may combine strips of different lengths as different whole modules, and consecutive layers may have different exact thicknesses.\n\nFor one exact layer thickness, choose a row type consisting of floor(S/module_width) complete identical strips across x. Its cost is the entire module row height, and its owner count is m times the strip count. Concatenating row types whose total height is at most S produces a physical layer; each row is centered across x, and all rows together are centered in y. Concatenating such physical layers whose full thicknesses sum to at most S gives a cube packing. The grammar's backpointer word decodes every strip, every t parity, every proper frame, and every box boundary. Disjoint interiors follow from full box separation between modules and the exact alternating-strip edge separation within each module.\n\nAll costs are(p+q*sqrt(3))/2 with nonnegative integer p,q, so the finite row and layer cost-state dynamic program applies directly. It retains whole strips and their complete boundary costs; a final named suffix of whole owners may be deleted while the full parent word is kept. Native and independent verification must reconstruct those actual owners and verify all host walls and pairs. The grammar is a construction family, not a completeness or optimality claim.\n",
   "cost_convention": "Each integer pair[p,q]denotes(p+q*sqrt(3))/2. Every module cost includes both complete strip ends.",
